Java Metaspace: The Memory Area That Can Crash Your Production JVM


Imagine this production incident:

2:37 AM — Payment Service is healthy.
CPU is normal.
Heap is only 55% full.
Suddenly the application starts failing with:

java.lang.OutOfMemoryError: Metaspace

The first question from the team is usually:

“But the heap isn't full. How can Java run out of memory?”

That's exactly why Metaspace deserves attention.


What is Metaspace?

Before Java 8, JVM class metadata was primarily stored in PermGen (Permanent Generation).

Java 8 removed PermGen and introduced Metaspace.

The important difference is:

Java Heap
 ├── Young Generation
 └── Old Generation

Native Memory
 ├── Metaspace
 ├── Compressed Class Space
 ├── Code Cache
 ├── Thread Stacks
 └── Other native allocations

So when you configure:

-Xmx2g

you are saying:

"The Java heap can grow up to 2 GB."

You are not saying:

"The JVM process can consume only 2 GB."

Metaspace lives outside the normal Java heap.


What actually goes into Metaspace?

When the JVM loads:

class PaymentService {
    void pay() {}
}

the .class file isn't simply copied into Metaspace.

The JVM creates internal metadata describing the class:

PaymentService
       │
       ├── class information
       ├── fields
       ├── methods
       ├── inheritance information
       ├── interfaces
       ├── runtime metadata
       └── JVM internal structures
                │
                ▼
            Metaspace

This is why applications that load many classes can consume significant Metaspace.

The ClassLoader is the real story

This is the part that matters most when troubleshooting.

A class isn't identified only by its name.

Conceptually:

Class name + Defining ClassLoader

means:

Employee loaded by Loader-A
        !=
Employee loaded by Loader-B

Even though both classes are called:

com.example.Employee

Therefore, if an application repeatedly creates ClassLoaders:

Loader-1 → 3,000 classes
Loader-2 → 3,000 classes
Loader-3 → 3,000 classes
Loader-4 → 3,000 classes
...

Static cache
     │
     ├── ClassLoader-1
     │      └── 3,000 classes
     │
     ├── ClassLoader-2
     │      └── 3,000 classes
     │
     ├── ClassLoader-3
     │      └── 3,000 classes
     │
     └── ClassLoader-4
            └── 3,000 classes

If those old ClassLoaders are still referenced, the JVM cannot unload their classes.


How do you troubleshoot it?

Don't immediately increase:

-XX:MaxMetaspaceSize

First determine whether the application genuinely needs more Metaspace or is leaking classes.

1. Check JVM configuration
jcmd <PID> VM.flags
jcmd <PID> VM.command_line

Look for:

-XX:MaxMetaspaceSize

2. Check class loading
jstat -class <PID> 5000

Watch:

Loaded
Unloaded

If loaded classes keep climbing:

18,000
22,000
28,000
35,000

Use:

jcmd <PID> VM.classloader_stats

Then ask four questions:

1. How much Metaspace is available?

2. Are loaded classes continuously increasing?

3. Are ClassLoaders accumulating?

4. Does the post-GC Metaspace baseline keep increasing?

Those four questions can turn a frightening production OOM into a structured investigation.